param u8 bit0 = !(1 <<8u 0);
param u8 bit1 = !(1 <<8u 1);
param u8 bit2 = !(1 <<8u 2);
param u8 bit3 = !(1 <<8u 3);
param u8 bit4 = !(1 <<8u 4);
param u8 bit5 = !(1 <<8u 5);
param u8 bit6 = !(1 <<8u 6);
param u8 bit7 = !(1 <<8u 7);

namespace WheelElem {

  /* Packed into 32 bits, 29 of them used:
       bits  0..7   unsetBit, the byte mask ~(1 << b)
       bits  8..11  nextMultipleFactor, at most 10
       bits 16..28  correctNext = (correct << wheelIndexBits) | next,
                    correct at most 10 and next at most 8*phi(WHEEL)-1 = 383

     correct and next share one field on purpose. The sieving loop ends with

         multipleIndex += correct;
         sievingPrime   = (sievePrime << 32) | (multipleIndex << 9) | next

     and 9 is exactly SievingPrime::wheelIndexBits, the width of the field next
     lands in. So

         (multipleIndex << 9) + correctNext

     adds correct and inserts next in one go: next is below bit 9, it cannot
     carry into the multipleIndex part, and correct sits at precisely the
     weight multipleIndex has once shifted. Extracting two fields, adding one
     and or-ing the other - seven instructions - becomes one shift and one add.
     See EratBig::crossOff.

     The rest of the order is chosen for the extraction, not for readability.
     unsetBit is in the low byte because it goes straight to the sieve, on the
     critical path from the table load; correctNext is at the top because
     nothing sits above it, so a plain shift extracts it with no mask. Only
     nextMultipleFactor is left needing a mask.

     One u32 load, not one load per field: the fields are deliberately NOT byte
     aligned. Byte aligning them (commit "pack wheel elements into 32 bits" +
     the byte-load variant) saves three ALU ops but costs two extra loads, and
     measured 1 to 2% SLOWER - this loop is bound by its memory accesses (a
     random read-modify-write on the sieve, plus the bucket read and write),
     not by the ALU.

     This type is only the element type of the generated tables. decompose
     takes a u64, because the caller widens at the load: `(64u)wheel[i]`
     compiles to a plain 32-bit load, which zero-extends for free, whereas
     widening a u32 already sitting in a register costs a separate move. */
  type t = u32;

  inline fn decompose (reg u64 elem) -> reg u8, reg u64, reg u64 {
    /* u8, so that it reaches the sieve as the low byte of the loaded entry
       with no extraction at all. */
    reg u8 unsetBit = (8u) elem;
    reg u64 nextMultipleFactor = elem; nextMultipleFactor >>u= 8; nextMultipleFactor &= (1<<4) - 1;
    reg u64 correctNext = elem; correctNext >>u= 16;
    return unsetBit, nextMultipleFactor, correctNext;
  }

}
